'''''Zanthoxylum''''' is a [[genus]] of about 250 species of [[deciduous]] and [[evergreen]] trees, shrubs and climbers in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Rutaceae]] that are native to warm [[temperate]] and [[Subtropics|subtropical]] areas worldwide. It is the type genus of the tribe Zanthoxyleae in the subfamily [[Rutoideae#Zanthoxyleae|Rutoideae]]. Several of the species have yellow [[Wood#Heartwood and sapwood|heartwood]], to which their generic name alludes.<ref>{{cite book|last1=Thomas|first1=Val|title=Sappi tree spotting: Highlands: Highveld, Drakensberg, Eastern Cape mountains|year=2001 |publisher=Jacana |location=Johannesburg |isbn=978-1-77009-561-8 |page=260 |edition=3rd |first2=Rina |last2=Grant |others=illustrations: Joan van Gogh; photographs: Jaco Adendorff}}</ref> Several species are cultivated for their use as spices, notably including [[Sichuan pepper]].

==Description==
Plants in the genus ''Zanthoxylum'' are typically [[dioecious]] shrubs, trees or woody climbers armed with [[Trichome#Plant trichomes|trichomes]]. The leaves are arranged alternately and are usually [[Pinnation|pinnate]] or [[Glossary of leaf morphology#trifoliate|trifoliate]]. The flowers are usually arranged in [[panicle]]s and usually function as male or female flowers with four [[sepal]]s and four petals, the sepals remaining attached to the fruit. Male flowers have four [[stamen]]s opposite the sepals. Female flowers have up to five, more or less free [[Gynoecium#Carpels|carpels]] with the [[Style (botany)|styles]] free or sometimes fused near the tip. The fruit is usually of up to four [[Follicle (fruit)|follicles]] fused at the base, each containing a single seed almost as large as the follicle.<ref name="RBGS">{{cite web |title=Genus ''Zanthoxylum'' |url=http://plantnet.rbgsyd.nsw.gov.au/cgi-bin/NSWfl.pl?page=nswfl&showsyn=&dist=&constat=&lvl=gn&name=Zanthoxylum |publisher=Royal Botanic Garden Sydney |access-date=17 August 2020}}</ref><ref name="FoA">{{cite book |last1=Hartley |first1=Thomas G. |editor-last1=Wilson |editor-first1=Annette J.G. |title=Flora of Australia (Volume 26) |date=2013 |publisher=Australian Biological Resources Study |location=Canberra |page=74 |url=https://profiles.ala.org.au/opus/foa/profile/Zanthoxylum |access-date=17 August 2020}}</ref>

==Taxonomy==
The genus ''Zanthoxylum'' was first formally described in 1753 by [[Carl Linnaeus]] in the first volume of ''[[Species Plantarum]]''.<ref name=APNI>{{cite web|title=''Zanthoxylum'' L. |url=https://id.biodiversity.org.au/name/apni/81376 |work=Australian Plant Name Index (APNI) |access-date=16 March 2026 }}</ref><ref name="L.">{{cite book |last1=Linnaeus |first1=Carl |title=Species Plantarum |date=1753 |publisher=Junk, 1908 |location=Berlin |page=270 |url=https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/84235#page/284/mode/1up |access-date=17 August 2020}}</ref>
The generic name is derived from [[Ancient Greek]] words {{wikt-lang|grc|ξανθός}} ({{transliteration|grc|xanthos}}), meaning 'yellow', and {{wikt-lang|grc|ξύλον}} ({{transliteration|grc|xylon}}), meaning 'wood'. It is technically misspelled, as the ''z'' should be ''x'', but botanical nomenclature does not allow for spelling corrections. It refers to a yellow dye made from the roots of some species.<ref>{{cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=zIOvJSJs-IkC |first=Umberto |last=Quattrocchi |title=CRC World Dictionary of Plant Names |volume=IV R-Z |year=2000 |publisher=Taylor & Francis US |isbn=978-0-8493-2678-3 |page=2868}}</ref> The first species that Linnaeus described was ''Zanthoxylum trifoliatum'', now regarded as a [[Synonym (taxonomy)|synonym]] of ''[[Eleutherococcus trifoliatus]]''.<ref name="L." /><ref name="POWO2">{{cite web |title=''Zanthoxylum trifoliatum'' |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/results?q=Zanthoxylum+trifoliatum |publisher=Plants of the World Online |access-date=17 August 2020}}</ref> The once separate genus ''Fagara'' is now included in ''Zanthoxylum''.<ref>{{cite journal|last=Beurton|first=C.|title=Gynoecium and perianth in ''Zanthoxylum'' s.l. (Rutaceae)|journal=Plant Systematics and Evolution|year=1994|volume=189|issue=3–4|pages=165–191|doi=10.1007/bf00939724|bibcode=1994PSyEv.189..165B |s2cid=2655415}}</ref>

==Fossil record==
28 [[fossil]] seeds of the extinct ''Zanthoxylum kristinae'', from the early [[Miocene]], have been found in the Kristina Mine at [[Hrádek nad Nisou]] in [[North Bohemia]], the Czech Republic.<ref>A review of the early Miocene Mastixioid flora of the Kristina Mine at Hrádek nad Nisou in North Bohemia, The Czech Republic, January 2012 by F. Holý, Z. Kvaček and Vasilis Teodoridis - ACTA MUSEI NATIONALIS PRAGAE Series B – Historia Naturalis • vol. 68 • 2012 • no. 3–4 • pp. 53–118</ref>

==Uses==
Many ''Zanthoxylum'' species make excellent [[bonsai]] and in temperate climates they can be grown quite well indoors. ''[[Zanthoxylum beecheyanum]]'' and ''[[Zanthoxylum piperitum]]'' are two species commonly grown as [[bonsai]].<ref name ="Wilbur" >Wilbur, C. Keith, MD. ''Revolutionary Medicine 1700-1800''. The Globe Pequot Press. Page 23. 1980.</ref>

===Culinary use===
[[Spice]]s are made from a number of species in this genus, including:
* ''[[Zanthoxylum acanthopodium]]'' – [[Andaliman]]
* ''[[Zanthoxylum armatum]]'' – "green" or "vine" [[Sichuan pepper]]
* ''[[Zanthoxylum bungeanum]]'' – "red" or common Sichuan pepper
* ''[[Zanthoxylum gilletii]]'' - Uzazi
* ''[[Zanthoxylum piperitum]]'' – {{transliteration|ja|[[Sansho (spice)|sanshō]]}}, {{transliteration|ko|[[Zanthoxylum piperitum#Korean cuisine|chopi]]}}
* ''[[Zanthoxylum nitidum]]''{{Citation needed|date=June 2022}}
* ''[[Zanthoxylum rhetsa]]'' – teppal, tirphal
* ''[[Zanthoxylum schinifolium]]'' – {{transliteration|ko|sancho}}, "green" Sichuan pepper
* ''[[Zanthoxylum simulans]]'' – "red" or wild Sichuan pepper

====Andaliman====
{{See also|Andaliman}}
In [[Indonesia]]'s [[North Sumatra]] province, ''[[Zanthoxylum acanthopodium]]'' is harvested for ''andaliman''.<ref>{{cite web |date=3 November 2006 |title=Andaliman – A Family of Sichuan Pepper |website=IndonesiaEats.com |url=https://indonesiaeats.com/andaliman-batak-pepper-indonesia-sichuan-pepper-family/ |access-date=15 October 2020 |archive-date=17 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201017033410/https://indonesiaeats.com/andaliman-batak-pepper-indonesia-sichuan-pepper-family/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> In Indonesian Batak cuisine, ''[[andaliman]]'' is ground and mixed with chilies and seasonings into a green [[sambal]] or chili paste.<ref>{{cite web |last=Leifsson |first=Lanny |date=8 November 2011 |title=Sambal Andaliman Recipe (Andaliman Pepper Sambal) |website=indonesiaeats.com |url=https://indonesiaeats.com/sambal-andaliman-batak-pepper-sambal/ |access-date=15 October 2020 |archive-date=30 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201030082455/https://indonesiaeats.com/sambal-andaliman-batak-pepper-sambal/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> [[Arsik]] is a typical Indonesian dish containing ''andaliman''.<ref>{{cite web |date=12 July 2012 |title=Arsik Recipe (Spiced Carp with Torch Ginger and Andaliman – Mandailing Style) |website=indonesiaeats.com |url=https://indonesiaeats.com/arsik-indonesian-andaliman-spiced-carp-batak/ |access-date=15 October 2020 |archive-date=17 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201017132159/https://indonesiaeats.com/arsik-indonesian-andaliman-spiced-carp-batak/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

===={{transliteration|ko|Chopi}} and {{transliteration|ja|sanshō}}====
{{See also|Zanthoxylum piperitum#Korean cuisine|Zanthoxylum piperitum#Japanese cuisine}}

''[[Zanthoxylum piperitum]]'' is harvested in Japan and Korea to produce {{transliteration|ja|[[sanshō (spice)|sanshō]]}} ({{lang|ja|山椒}}) or {{transliteration|ko|[[Zanthoxylum piperitum#Korean cuisine|chopi]]}} ({{lang|ko|초피}}), which has numbing properties similar to those of Chinese Sichuan peppercorns.<ref>{{cite encyclopedia |last=Ravindran |first=P. N. |author-link=P. N. Ravindran |year=2017 |title=100 Japanese Pepper Zanthoxylum piperitum |encyclopedia=The Encyclopedia of Herbs and Spices |publisher=[[CAB International]] |pages=473–476 |isbn=978-1-780-64315-1 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=6pJNDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA476}}</ref>
In Korean cuisine, {{transliteration|ko|chopi}} is often used to accompany fish soups such as {{transliteration|ko|chueo-tang}}, whereas the plant's seeds are separated and used to make oil, and the oil is used as a medicine.

====Sancho====
{{See also|Sancho (spice)}}
The Korean {{transliteration|ko|[[Sancho (spice)|sancho]]}} is made from ''[[Zanthoxylum schinifolium]]'', which is slightly less bitter than {{transliteration|ko|chopi}}.<ref name=graz>{{cite web |date=17 June 2020 |title=Szechuan Peppercorns) |website=CooksInfo.com |url=https://www.cooksinfo.com/szechuan-peppercorns |access-date=15 October 2020}}</ref> In [[Cuisine of Korea|Korean cuisine]], {{transliteration|ko|chopi}} is often used to accompany fish soups such as {{transliteration|ko|[[chueo-tang]]}}.<ref>{{cite web |last=Gowman |first=Philip |date=5 December 2016 |title=In praise of Sancho and Flat Three |website=londonkoreanlinks.net |url=https://londonkoreanlinks.net/2016/12/05/in-praise-of-sancho-and-flat-three/ |access-date=15 October 2020}}</ref>

====Sichuan pepper====
{{See also|Sichuan pepper}}
The [[fruit]] of ''[[Zanthoxylum armatum]]'' and ''[[Zanthoxylum bungeanum]]'' species is used to make [[Sichuan pepper]] by grinding the husks that surround the berries.<ref>{{cite book |title=Handbook of Herbs and Spices |volume=2 |first=K. V. |last=Peter |year=2004 |isbn=978-1-85573-721-1 |publisher=Woodhead Publishing |pages=98–99}}</ref>

====Tirphal and teppal====
In the states of [[Maharashtrian cuisine|Maharashtra]], [[Cuisine of Karnataka|Karnataka]], and [[Goan cuisine|Goa]] in Western India, the dried berries of ''[[Zanthoxylum rhetsa]]'' are known as teppal or tirphal in [[Marathi language|Marathi]] are added to foods such as legumes and fish. The name in both languages means 'three fruits' or 'three pods'.<ref>{{cite web |title=Tirphal/ Teppal Pepper |website=foodsofnations.com |url=https://foodsofnations.com/product/tirphal-teppal-pepper/ |access-date=15 October 2020 |archive-date=27 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201027123033/https://foodsofnations.com/product/tirphal-teppal-pepper/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> Because the trees bear fruit during the [[monsoon]] season, the berries are associated with the concurrent [[Krishna Janmashtami]] festival.<ref>{{cite book |title=Indian Spice Kitchen |first=Monisha |last=Bharadwaj |publisher=Hippocrene Books |year=2006 |isbn=978-0-7818-1143-9 |pages=82–83}}</ref>

The fresh fruits are parrot green in color and are used as a flavouring agent in many curries made with a paste of coconut, chilis, and other spices. When dried, the flesh of the fruit hardens, turns a brownish black color and opens up to show the black seeds within. The seeds are discarded and the dried fruit is stored in containers for use around the year. Mostly used in fish preparations and a few vegetarian dishes, with coconut masala, this spice has a very strong woody aroma and is discarded at the time of eating the curry.

==Chemistry==
Plants in the genus ''Zanthoxylum'' contain the lignan [[sesamin]].

Species identified in Nigeria contains several types of alkaloids including [[benzophenanthridine]]s ([[nitidine]], dihydronitidine, oxynitidine, [[fagaronine]], dihydro[[avicine]], [[chelerythrine]], dihydrochelerythrine, methoxychelerythrine, norchelerythrine, oxychelerythrine, [[decarine]] and [[fagaridine]]), [[furoquinoline]]s ([[dictamine]], 8-methoxydictamine, [[skimmianine]], 3-dimethylallyl-4-methoxy-2-quinolone), [[carbazole]]s (3-methoxycarbazole, [[glycozoline]]), [[aporphine]]s ([[berberine]], [[tembetarine]],<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://kanaya.naist.jp/knapsack_jsp/information.jsp?word=C00025347 |title={title} |access-date=2017-05-18 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171217135728/http://kanaya.naist.jp/knapsack_jsp/information.jsp?word=C00025347 |archive-date=2017-12-17 |url-status=live }}</ref> [[magnoflorine]], M-methyl-[[corydine]]), [[canthinone]]s (6-canthinone), [[acridone]]s (1-hydroxy-3-methoxy-10-methylacridon-9-one, 1-hydroxy-10-methylacridon-9-one, [[zanthozolin]]), and aromatic and [[aliphatic amide]]s.<ref>The Nigerian Zanthoxylum; Chemical and biological values. S. K. Adesina, Afr. J. Trad. CAM, 2005, volume 2, issue 3, pages 282-301 ([https://tspace.library.utoronto.ca/bitstream/1807/9214/1/tc05032.pdf article] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303183649/https://tspace.library.utoronto.ca/bitstream/1807/9214/1/tc05032.pdf |date=2016-03-03 }})</ref>

[[Hydroxy-alpha sanshool]] is a bioactive component of plants from the genus ''Zanthoxylum'', including the Sichuan pepper. It provides the characteristic numbness.

==Ecology==
''Zanthoxylum'' species are used as food plants by the [[Caterpillar|larvae]] of some [[Lepidoptera]] species including the [[Engrailed (moth)|engrailed]] (''Ectropis crepuscularia'').

